        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <BR>
The goals for atherosclerotic screening of astronauts in preparation for space exploration are quite different than that used for screening of the general population. Since an in-flight atherosclerotic complication would put the individual and the entire mission at great peril, any screening algorithm is required to definitively identify the predisposition to future atherothrombic events irrespective of the presence of significant stenosis. The aim of this proposal is to use state-of-the-art "omics" approaches for improving atherosclerotic risk assessment in apparently healthy individuals. This proposal is divided into two main aims; first to identify representative individuals with various risk for cardiovascular outcomes and second to use personal metabolomics and lipidomics profiling to correlate risk of disease with molecular biomarkers. <P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Risk will be stratified in a two-step process, first involving the recruitment of asymptomatic subjects with either very low, moderate, or very high risk features on coronary computed tomography (CT) angiography features. A second evaluation method (myocardial contrast echocardiography) will then be applied to assess endothelial function within each cohort since the vascular endothelium is a key target in the pathophysiology of accelerated atherosclerotic disease during deep space exploration. For each risk category group, an integrative “omic” analysis from serum and peripheral blood monocytes will be performed, which will focus on differences in unbiased metabolomics, lipidomic, and oxidative proteomics using mass spectroscopy and ultra performance liquid chromatography (UPLC). In addition, whole genomic sequencing will be performed on peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) from as many as 20 individuals for use in a disease risk evaluation. Any “omics" patterns that are identified as a strong predictor of risk may be particularly useful for identifying individuals with heightened risk for the effects of prolonged space exploration. These tests may not only be useful for initial screening, but also for periodic evaluation during mission lead time and also for investigational assessment of the effects of space radiation on the International Space Station (ISS) or during space exploration missions. To perform these studies, a multidisciplinary team of investigators with expertise in non-invasive cardiovascular imaging in risk assessment, metabolomics, proteomics, endothelial biology and bioinformatics/biostatistics has been assembled.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
The results for this experiment are currently not available.
        </div>

